+// SK-FETCHRETRY (2026-11-23): one retry for a fetch that failed, on a fresh git channel.
+//
+// A conversation keeps one git channel per peer device and reuses it. When the peer's process is
+// killed (an app update, a crash, the OOM killer) nothing closes that channel: the death is only
+// discovered by the first write. If that first write is the fetch of a commit the peer's NEW process
+// has just announced over a NEW connection, the fetch fails on the dead channel, the channel is
+// dropped -- and upstream stops there, logging "Relaunch sync" without relaunching anything. The
+// commit then waits for the next unrelated notification. Measured 2026-11-23: a message announced
+// and fetchable over a connection 80 ms old sat for five minutes, because the fetch went down the
+// 43-second-old channel to the killed process.
+//
+// The retry drops the cached channel for that device first, so it asks the connection manager for a
+// new one: on whatever live connection exists, or a fresh dial. Once only; a second failure ends
+// as before. Lines go to SK-SWARMSYNC (Settings -> Diagnostics -> Swarm sync, group 32).
+#ifdef __ANDROID__
+#include <android/log.h>
+extern "C" int sk_diag_on(int group);
+#define SK_FR(...) do { if (sk_diag_on(32)) __android_log_print(ANDROID_LOG_ERROR, "SK-SWARMSYNC", __VA_ARGS__); } while (0)
+#else
+#define SK_FR(...) do { } while (0)
+#endif
+
